### What are BiConsumer, BiPredicate, BiFunction?

### BiConsumer: two inputs, no return

```java
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.function.BiConsumer;

Map<String, BigDecimal> balances = Map.of(
    "SAV2001", new BigDecimal("125000"),
    "CUR3001", new BigDecimal("850000"));

// Map.forEach takes BiConsumer<K, V>
balances.forEach((accountId, balance) -> {
    System.out.println("Account " + accountId + ": Rs." + balance);
});

// Composition: andThen chains two BiConsumers
BiConsumer<String, BigDecimal> logIt = (id, bal) -> System.out.println(id + ": " + bal);
BiConsumer<String, BigDecimal> auditIt = (id, bal) -> auditService.record(id, bal);
balances.forEach(logIt.andThen(auditIt));
```

### BiFunction: two inputs, one return

```java
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

// Map.merge: BiFunction resolves key collision
Map<String, BigDecimal> totals = new HashMap<>();
trades.forEach(t -> {
    totals.merge(t.getSymbol(), t.getAmount(), BigDecimal::add);
});
// merge(key, newValue, BiFunction<existing, newValue, result>)

// Map.compute: BiFunction computes from key + existing value
balances.compute("SAV2001", (key, existing) -> {
    return existing == null ? BigDecimal.ZERO : existing.add(interest);
});

// Map.replaceAll: BiFunction transforms all values
balances.replaceAll((accountId, balance) ->
    balance.multiply(new BigDecimal("1.05")));  // 5% interest
```

### BiPredicate: two inputs, boolean return

```java
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.function.BiPredicate;

BiPredicate<Trade, BigDecimal> exceedsLimit = (trade, limit) ->
    trade.getAmount().compareTo(limit) > 0;

// Composition:
BiPredicate<Trade, BigDecimal> exceedsAndSettled = exceedsLimit
    .and((trade, limit) -> trade.isSettled());

boolean result = exceedsLimit.test(trade, new BigDecimal("100000"));
```

### What is the relationship to single-argument counterparts?

| Single | Bi | Difference |
|---|---|---|
| Consumer<T> | BiConsumer<T,U> | +1 input |
| Function<T,R> | BiFunction<T,U,R> | +1 input |
| Predicate<T> | BiPredicate<T,U> | +1 input |

### What should a senior engineer know?

- Map.merge with BigDecimal::add is the idiomatic way to aggregate by key in one line.
- Map.compute returns null to remove the entry, or a new value to update it.
- For three arguments, define a custom interface: `@FunctionalInterface interface TriFunction<A,B,C,R> { R apply(A a, B b, C c); }`.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- What Map methods use BiFunction? (merge, compute, computeIfPresent, replaceAll.)
- Can BiConsumer return a value? (No. Use BiFunction for that.)
- Is there a TriConsumer? (No. Define your own or use currying.)

### What are common mistakes?

```java
// WRONG: using Consumer when two inputs are needed
// Map.forEach needs BiConsumer, not Consumer
// balances.forEach(id -> System.out.println(id)); // COMPILE ERROR: expects (K, V)

// CORRECT: BiConsumer accepts two inputs
balances.forEach((id, balance) -> System.out.println(id + ": Rs." + balance));
```
